          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"A week is fundamentally a sevenfold unit or heptad: ordinarily seven days, but in contexts such as Daniel 9 commonly understood as seven years. Its sevenfold form may also suggest completeness or divine order.\",\"book_summary\":\"A week is a unit of seven whose temporal scale may be days or years, depending on what is being counted.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A week is a heptad that can denote either seven days or, in appropriate prophetic or sabbatical contexts, seven years.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A week is a unit of seven applicable to days or years according to context.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The consensus discusses symbolic associations such as completeness and Sabbath order that the book omits, but the book does not reject them; it simply gives the basic definitional sense.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify the week as a sevenfold unit and recognize day-scale and year-scale applications.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum claims, the book and consensus make the same identification. The consensus often foregrounds Daniel's seven-year heptads, while the book states the broader underlying rule, but the consensus responses also recognize ordinary seven-day weeks and the contextual nature of the scale. The difference is emphasis rather than a changed referent or extension.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The printed excerpts support seven-day counting and seven-year sabbatical counting, but they do not explicitly state that Daniel's “weeks” are weeks of years. Genesis 29 distinguishes the bridal week from the subsequent seven years, and connecting Daniel 9 to year-scale heptads requires interpretive inference.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
